📚 Concepts Studied

1. Why Fast Algorithms Are Dangerous for Passwords
MD5 and SHA-256 were designed for speed — file checksums, digital
signatures, data integrity. That speed becomes a weapon when an attacker
gets a password database. A single consumer CPU can attempt 900,000+
MD5 guesses per second. Speed is a feature for checksums; it is a
vulnerability for passwords.

2. Dictionary Attacks
Not random guessing. The attacker takes a real list of passwords people
have used (rockyou.txt — 14.3 million entries from the 2009 RockYou breach)
and hashes each one, comparing to the stored hash. If they match, the
password is recovered. No cryptography is broken. It is just fast comparison.

3. Why bcrypt Is Different
bcrypt was designed specifically for passwords in 1999. Its defining feature
is a configurable work factor cost 12 means the algorithm runs 4,096
internal rounds deliberately. This makes every single guess take real time.
An attacker trying bcrypt gets only 3 guesses per second on the same hardware
that gives them 900,000 MD5 guesses per second.

4. Intel SHA-NI Hardware Acceleration
Modern Intel CPUs (8th gen+) include SHA hardware extension instructions
that accelerate SHA-256 computation at the silicon level. This caused
SHA-256 to run faster than MD5 in Hashcat on this lab's CPU. The lesson:
hardware improvements available to defenders are equally available to
attackers. SHA-256 is still completely wrong for passwords regardless.

5. Work Factor and Computational Cost
The work factor in bcrypt controls how slow the algorithm is — and it can
be increased as hardware gets faster. This is called adaptive hashing.
MD5 and SHA-256 do not have this. They were fast in 1991 and they are still
exactly as fast today, but hardware has become millions of times more powerful.

6. CVSS Risk Scoring
Learned how to use the CVSS v3.1 framework to rate a vulnerability's
severity across 8 metrics — attack vector, complexity, privileges required,
user interaction, scope, and three impact dimensions. Calculated a score
of 9.1 (Critical) for MD5/SHA-256 password storage.

7. CWE and OWASP Classification
CWE-916 covers the use of password hashes with insufficient computational
effort. OWASP classifies this under A02:2021 — Cryptographic Failures,
their second most critical web application vulnerability category.

8. Client-Facing Security Reporting
Learned how to translate a technical lab finding into a professional
client document — non-technical executive summary, evidence-backed
technical findings, CVSS rating, and step-by-step remediation. The goal
is not to impress technically. The goal is to make a non-technical
executive understand the risk and know exactly what to do.


