Hashcat Modes Used

bash
# Generate hashes
while read p; do echo -n "$p" | md5sum | awk '{print $1}'; done < passwords.txt > hashes_md5.txt
while read p; do echo -n "$p" | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}'; done < passwords.txt > hashes_sha256.txt

# Crack
hashcat -m 0    -a 0 hashes_md5.txt    rockyou.txt --force -o cracked_md5.txt
hashcat -m 1400 -a 0 hashes_sha256.txt rockyou.txt --force -o cracked_sha256.txt
hashcat -m 3200 -a 0 hashes_bcrypt.txt rockyou.txt --force -o cracked_bcrypt.txt

# Mode reference
# 0    = MD5
# 1400 = SHA-256
# 3200 = bcrypt
```

🔍 Methodology

1. Created 10 sample passwords ranging from very weak to strong
2. Generated MD5, SHA-256, and bcrypt (cost 12) hash files from the same list
3. Ran Hashcat against all three using rockyou.txt wordlist — dictionary attack only
4. Ran John the Ripper on all three for cross-verification
5. Recorded speed (H/s), passwords cracked, and time for each algorithm
6. Calculated speed differential between MD5/SHA-256 and bcrypt
7. Mapped findings to CWE-916 and OWASP A02:2021
8. Scored with CVSS v3.1 calculator
9. Wrote client-ready report: executive summary, technical findings, CVSS rating, remediation
10. Wrote SafeX outreach email template (generic/educational — no real orgs named)



