🧠 Key Learnings

- MD5 cracks a 14-million-entry wordlist in 83 seconds — it has zero place in password storage
- SHA-256 was actually faster than MD5 on this CPU due to Intel SHA-NI hardware acceleration — hardware improvements help attackers too
- bcrypt at cost 12 limits an attacker to 3 guesses per second — the slowness is the security
- The two passwords that survived all attacks (Summer2023, Tr0ub4dor3) were not in the wordlist — a rule-based attack would likely recover them from MD5/SHA-256 within minutes
- CVSS scoring is not just a number — each metric tells a story about the real-world path an attacker would take
- Writing for a non-technical executive is harder than writing for a developer — clarity matters more than accuracy of terminology
- SHA-NI is a real factor: algorithm speed comparisons depend heavily on hardware and tool implementation, not just the algorithm design


